The contract is clear. I need to implement two methods:

1. `public String buildJdbcUrl(String dbType, String host, int port, String databaseName, String extraParams)` — builds `jdbc:postgresql://host:port/db` if dbType equals "POSTGRESQL" case-insensitively, else `jdbc:mysql://host:port/db`; appends `?extraParams` if extraParams is non-null and non-blank.

2. `public ConnectionTestResult testConnection(String dbType, String host, int port, String databaseName, String username, String password, String extraParams)` — builds URL, measures wall-clock, attempts `DriverManager.getConnection(jdbcUrl, username, password)`, closes on success, returns ConnectionTestResult. SQLException → success=false with message. Never propagates.

Let me write the replacement. The masked region is between the class opening and `executeQuery`. I'll replace `<MASKED>` with the two methods.

Implementation:

```java
    public String buildJdbcUrl(String dbType, String host, int port, String databaseName, String extraParams) {
        String baseUrl;
        if ("POSTGRESQL".equalsIgnoreCase(dbType)) {
            baseUrl = "jdbc:postgresql://" + host + ":" + port + "/" + databaseName;
        } else {
            baseUrl = "jdbc:mysql://" + host + ":" + port + "/" + databaseName;
        }
        if (extraParams != null && !extraParams.isBlank()) {
            baseUrl += "?" + extraParams;
        }
        return baseUrl;
    }

    public ConnectionTestResult testConnection(String dbType, String host, int port,
                                               String databaseName, String username, String password, String extraParams) {
        String jdbcUrl = buildJdbcUrl(dbType, host, port, databaseName, extraParams);
        long startTime = System.currentTimeMillis();
        try (Connection conn = DriverManager.getConnection(jdbcUrl, username, password)) {
            long responseTimeMs = System.currentTimeMillis() - startTime;
            return new ConnectionTestResult(true, jdbcUrl, null, responseTimeMs);
        } catch (SQLException e) {
            long responseTimeMs = System.currentTimeMillis() - startTime;
            return new ConnectionTestResult(false, jdbcUrl, e.getMessage(), responseTimeMs);
        }
    }
```

Note: try-with-resources closes the connection before the result is built — actually the resource is closed when the try block exits, which happens before the return statement completes... Actually in try-with-resources, resources are closed before the return value is returned (the return expression is evaluated, then resources closed, then the value returned). The contract says "an established connection is closed before returning" — try-with-resources satisfies this. And "on success the connection is closed before the result is built" — hmm, strictly the result is built (constructor called) before the close in try-with-resources semantics. To be safe and match the contract literally, I could use explicit close:

```java
        Connection conn = null;
        try {
            conn = DriverManager.getConnection(jdbcUrl, username, password);
            long responseTimeMs = System.currentTimeMillis() - startTime;
            conn.close();
            return new ConnectionTestResult(true, jdbcUrl, null, responseTimeMs);
        } catch (SQLException e) {
            ...
        } finally {
            if (conn != null) try { conn.close(); } catch (SQLException ignored) {}
        }
```

Simpler: use try-with-resources but build result after close explicitly:

```java
        long startTime = System.currentTimeMillis();
        try (Connection conn = DriverManager.getConnection(jdbcUrl, username, password)) {
            long responseTimeMs = System.currentTimeMillis() - startTime;
            conn.close();  // explicit close before building result
            return new ConnectionTestResult(true, jdbcUrl, null, responseTimeMs);
        } catch (SQLException e) {
            long responseTimeMs = System.currentTimeMillis() - startTime;
            return new ConnectionTestResult(false, jdbcUrl, e.getMessage(), responseTimeMs);
        }
```

Calling conn.close() explicitly inside try-with-resources is fine (double close is a no-op). This satisfies "closed before the result is built". Duration brackets the attempt in both branches — yes, measured after getConnection returns/throws.

The contract says "the duration brackets the attempt in both outcome branches" — start before getConnection, measure after. Good.

